<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mary Seacole, a 71-year-old woman with hypertension treated with amlodipine and chlorthalidone for eight weeks, came the same day because she has felt dizzy whenever she stands up for two days. She has had a head cold for four days, with a runny nose, a sore throat and little appetite, and has eaten mostly tea and toast. This morning, getting up from her chair, the room went grey and she caught the kitchen table; she did not fall, hit anything or lose consciousness. She has kept taking both blood pressure tablets, because she did not think a cold counted as being unable to eat; she has had no vomiting or diarrhea. She has no confusion, headache, chest pain, palpitations, weakness or numbness. She has not taken liquorice, which she paused at her first visit, or any cold remedies from the chemist. As a nurse she noticed her own pulse was fast and her mouth dry. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days of dizziness on standing; cold for four days</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Near-fall this morning; caught the table; no injury</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Little food for four days; still taking chlorthalidone and amlodipine</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Dry mouth, fast pulse; no confusion, vomiting, diarrhea, chest pain or neurological symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better lying down or sitting</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

