    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Thiazide-associated hyponatremia and hypokalemia with orthostatic hypotension (E87.1)</content>. Sodium 129 mmol/L, potassium 3.3 mmol/L and creatinine 1.05 mg/dL (eGFR 57), from chlorthalidone together with four days of little food and salt, which leaves the kidneys unable to clear water; she is also low in volume, which causes the drop in pressure on standing. Mild and without confusion, so it can be managed at home with a recheck; chlorthalidone should not be restarted, since older women who develop thiazide hyponatremia often develop it again. A near-fall on a blood pressure medicine is exactly the risk the falls plan is meant to catch. [S]<list><item>Stop chlorthalidone for good; record thiazide hyponatremia as a reason not to use it again.</item><item>Continue amlodipine 5 mg.</item><item>Eat normally, with soup or broth and some salt for a few days, and fruit, vegetables and beans for potassium; drink to thirst rather than large amounts of tea or water.</item><item>Stand up slowly: sit on the edge of the bed or chair first; use the cane.</item><item>Sodium and potassium again in one week at the clinic laboratory.</item><item>Go to the emergency department for confusion, vomiting, a fall, fainting or a severe headache.</item><item>For the cold: rest, warm drinks, honey and ginger as she likes; no decongestant tablets, which raise blood pressure, and no anti-inflammatory cold remedies.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertension (I10)</content>. Pressure is low today because of volume loss; it will rise again off the diuretic. [S]<list><item>Home readings once daily; bring them to the three-month review.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); STEADI: Stopping Elderly Accidents, Deaths, and Injuries (2026); American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Laboratory recheck in one week. Nurse telephone call in two days. Three-month review in five weeks. Next scheduled visit 1877-06-15: three-month review after chlorthalidone was stopped: blood pressure above target, losartan added; lipids on atorvastatin; knee and falls. [S]</paragraph></text>

